Astropad today launched <a href="https://shop.astropad.com/collections/shop-astropad/products/rock-paper-pencil">Rock Paper Pencil 4.0</a>, an updated version of its screen protector and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-pencil/">Apple Pencil</a> tip combo made for the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<div class="center-wrap"><iframe title="Rock Paper Pencil 4.0" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/w70QaMbVFn8" width="560" height="315" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen"></iframe></div><br/>
Rock Paper Pencil offers a paper-like writing experience on a tablet display by pairing a paper-textured screen protector with custom tips for the &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj;. The new version of the product comes with two &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; tips, one with a smooth ballpoint feel and one that mimics a soft graphite pencil. Two of each tip come in the kit for four total.<br/>
<br/>
Astropad says the new graphite tip is meant to feel just like a 2B pencil with a "soft, toothy drag" that's ideal for sketching or shading. It even has the sound of a pencil on paper. The ballpoint tip is meant to feel like a smooth gel pen, and it's suited to writing and linework.<br/>
<br/>
The reusable screen protector remains the same as before, and users can put it on when writing or drawing on the &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; and take it off for other activities like gaming. It uses Astropad's NanoCling technology to attach, and it leaves no residue on the &zwnj;iPad&zwnj;'s display because it doesn't use adhesive. The paper-like feel of the screen protector comes from a microscopic texture that adds a small amount of friction to improve control. When not in use, the screen protector can be kept in an included protective storage sleeve.<br/>
<br/>
